[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Revocation lists (CRL) (/docs/pr-preview/pr-60/platform/pki/crl)\n\n\n\n<span id=\"crl-service\" />\n\nA CRL is an issuer-signed list of revoked certificates. This guide is for an operator publishing lists and an integrator using them locally. You need the issuing CA certificate, its <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">SKI</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">CA ID</ins> and access to the published endpoint. The client must maintain a current copy: offline operation still requires refreshing evidence.\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-del lm-diff-block\">\n  VA role and per-CA configuration\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  CRL settings [#crl-settings]\n</div>\n\nThe <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">VA role stores </del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">CRL settings of a CA store the </ins>configuration and metadata <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">for the</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">of its</ins> latest <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">list published for a CA’s SKI</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">published list. Each CA has its own settings, keyed by its CA ID — even when two CAs share a key: they share the SKI, not the CRL</ins>. Generation requires a usable issuer key; do not assume an imported CA without a key has an available CRL.\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Field        | Purpose                                                                                                                                         |\n  | ------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `ca_id`      | ID of the CA the settings belong to.                                                                                                            |\n  | `status`     | `ACTIVE` settings generate CRLs. `ARCHIVED` ones belong to a CA that no longer exists: their last CRL is still served, but never regenerated.   |\n  | `legacy_ski` | Read-only. SKI the settings were keyed by before the 4.0.0 migration; only migrated settings have it, and `/crl/<ski>` keeps resolving to them. |\n  | `latest_crl` | Version, validity start and end of the last published list. Read-only.                                                                          |\n</div>\n\n| `crl_options` field                                                                                                            | Purpose                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">Backend initial</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">Initial</ins> value |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `validity`                                                                                                                     | Time from `thisUpdate` to `nextUpdate`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | 7 days.                                                                                            |\n| `refresh_interval`                                                                                                             | Time since the issuance of the latest published CRL that triggers scheduled renewal; `0` disables only this trigger. The effective granularity is bounded by the job frequency (see [Periodic and automatic regeneration](#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration)). | 6 days and 23 hours.                                                                               |\n| `regenerate_on_revoke`                                                                                                         | Request regeneration on revocation or reactivation from `CertificateHold` events.                                                                                                                                                                                 | `true`.                                                                                            |\n| <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">`signer_certificate_serial_number`</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">`signer_serial_number`</ins> | Serial number of the existing certificate that signs the CRL.                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Serial number of the CA’s current certificate.                                                     |\n\nThe signer must be an existing certificate with the `cRLSign` key usage and the same subject and public key as the CA. Certificates from before a reissue qualify. A delegated signer is not supported: a certificate with the CA’s subject but a different key is rejected.\n\nWhen a CA is reissued and the <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">role’s </del>signer is the superseded CA certificate, the VA moves the signer to the new certificate — provided it carries `cRLSign` — and republishes the CRL. If the new certificate lacks `cRLSign`, the VA keeps the previous signer and logs a warning. If the signer is any other certificate with the CA’s subject and key, it is deliberately left unchanged; the previous certificate then cannot be deleted while the <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">role uses</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">settings use</ins> it (the API <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">rejects it with</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">returns</ins> `409` `certificate is in use`), and to retire it you update the signer with `PUT`.\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-del lm-diff-block\">\n  <Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"Cases in which the role can no longer be edited\">\n    When a CA is deleted, its VA role is kept and cannot be removed through the API. If no other CA with the same SKI remains, the role can be read but any `PUT` returns 404, and the renewal job logs a warning on every cycle because it no longer finds the CA. The API offers no way out; it can only be fixed by editing the database. If another CA with that SKI remains, the role is still editable (see [CAs that share a key](#cas-that-share-a-key)). Adjust validity and signer before deleting the CA.\n\n    A CA whose certificate lacks `cRLSign` cannot generate its CRL or edit its role (400 error). Since 4.0 the VA requires this usage. Check before importing with `openssl x509 -noout -ext keyUsage` and, if it is missing, obtain a certificate for the same CA and key that includes it and set it as the signer with `PUT`. After reissuing a CA, check the key usage of the new certificate: if it lacks `cRLSign`, the VA keeps the previous signer and logs a warning, so the lists keep being signed by the superseded certificate until you fix the usage.\n\n    `PUT` always validates the signer, even when the request does not change it. If the stored signer is no longer valid, no field of the role can be modified, not even the validity, until a valid one is set.\n  </Callout>\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A CA whose signer lacks `cRLSign` cannot generate its CRL, and an update that selects a signer without `cRLSign` is rejected (400 error). Since 4.0 the VA requires this usage. Check before importing with `openssl x509 -noout -ext keyUsage` and, if it is missing, obtain a certificate for the same CA and key that includes it and update the settings to use it as the signer. After reissuing a CA, also check the key usage of the new certificate: it may not keep the original’s usages, and without `cRLSign` the settings keep their previous signer.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  <Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"Cases in which the settings can no longer be edited\">\n    Deleting a CA keeps its CRL settings, and they cannot be removed through the API. What happens next depends on when the CA was deleted:\n\n    * The 4.0.0 upgrade migration archives the settings of the CAs that had been deleted before the upgrade. Their `status` is `ARCHIVED` and their `ca_id` is `legacy-<ski>`: the last CRL keeps being served, but it is never regenerated, any `PUT` returns 409, and there is no API way back to `ACTIVE`.\n    * A CA deleted while running 4.0.0 leaves `ACTIVE` settings. The last CRL keeps being served, but once it expires nothing regenerates it: the renewal job logs `something went wrong while calculating CRL for CA <ca-id>` on every cycle because it can no longer resolve the CA. The options can still be edited while the signer is unchanged; changing the signer fails, because there is no CA left to match it.\n\n    Adjust validity and signer before deleting a CA.\n\n    `PUT` validates the signer only when the request changes it. If the stored signer is no longer valid, the other options can still be edited, but the CRL cannot be regenerated until a valid signer is set.\n  </Callout>\n</div>\n\n<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"Do not share keys between CAs\">\n  Sharing a key between CAs <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">can prevent CRL validation</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">is a migration blocker: the 4.0.0 upgrade aborts while an SKI belongs to more than one CA</ins>. See [CAs that share a key](#cas-that-share-a-key).\n</Callout>\n\n<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">`latest_crl` contains version, validity start and end. Versions</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">CRL versions</ins> are kept as PEM in object storage at <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">`pki/va/crl/{subject_key_id}/{version}.crl`</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">`pki/va/crl/<ca-id>/<version>.crl`</ins>; the public endpoint returns DER<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">. CRLs published before the 4.0.0 upgrade remain at `pki/va/crl/<ski>/<version>.crl` and keep being served</ins>.\n\nCAs that share a key [#cas-that-share-a-key]\n\n<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">The VA identifies each CRL by the CA’s SKI, and the SKI is derived from its key. Two CAs with the same key have the same SKI and share a single VA role and a single CRL. This happens when a CA is created on an existing KMS key, or when a CA is imported whose certificate declares an SKI already in use. Lamassu takes the SKI of an imported certificate as-is and does not check that it corresponds to its public key. The first CA creates the role and the others keep it</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">Since 4.0.0, each CA publishes its own CRL even when its key is shared: two CAs with the same key have the same SKI, but each has its own CRL settings and its own list, signed by a certificate with that CA’s identity</ins>.\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Certificates issued from now on carry `/crl/<ca-id>` distribution points, so each CA’s certificates resolve to its own list. Certificates issued before the upgrade keep their `/crl/<ski>` distribution points: that URL keeps resolving to the settings the upgrade migrated, and always serves the list of the CA that owned that SKI when the upgrade ran. It is never rebound to a CA created later with the same key. After regeneration, the migrated list includes both URLs in its *Issuing Distribution Point* (OID `2.5.29.28`), so a verifier that compares the IDP with the CDP of the certificate accepts either form. Until the VA scheduled CRL job successfully regenerates it, the pre-upgrade list contains only the legacy `/crl/<ski>` IDP; IDP-checking verifiers cannot validate newly issued certificates with `/crl/<ca-id>` CDPs. The first run can take up to about an hour with the default schedule, and failures can extend the window.\n</div>\n\n<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">The certificates of all of them point to `/crl/{ski}`, but the published list has the first CA as issuer and only includes its revocations. A verifier compares the CRL issuer with the issuer of the certificate it validates (RFC 5280, §6.3.2) and rejects the list if they differ. Depending on its policy, the certificate is blocked (</del>*<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">hard-fail</del>*<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">) or accepted even though it is revoked (</del>*<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">soft-fail</del>*<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">). Revocations of the secondary CAs do not appear in any list</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">Sharing an SKI between CAs blocks the upgrade: the migration aborts and names the SKIs. To detect the problem before upgrading, compare the SKI of the CAs in the inventory: two with the same SKI are affected. To fix it, create a CA with its own key and reissue its certificates; those already issued keep the old URL and are not corrected until reissued</ins>.\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-del lm-diff-block\">\n  If the first CA is deleted, CRL generation for the remaining one fails because the configured signer no longer matches a CA with that name. Until the role’s signer is updated to a certificate of the remaining CA, the last published list keeps being served until it expires (7 days by default).\n</div>\n\n<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">Before upgrading, review CAs that share a key. To detect the problem, compare the SKI of the CAs in the inventory: two with the same SKI are affected. To fix it, create a CA with its own key and reissue its certificates; those already issued keep the old URL and are not corrected until reissued. A CA imported with an SKI already in use does not replace the existing one as CRL issuer, because the issuer is resolved from the role’s signer. However, its own certificates point to that same URL and its revocations are not published. Until Lamassu rejects these imports,</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">Lamassu takes the SKI of an imported certificate as-is and does not check that it corresponds to its public key. Until Lamassu rejects imports whose SKI collides with an existing CA — proposed in </ins>[<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">lamassuiot#834</ins>](https://github.com/lamassuiot/lamassuiot/issues/834)<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">, together with the per-CA CRL that 4.0.0 implements —</ins> restrict the permission to import CAs as an interim control; it covers only the import path. Creating a CA with **Create New CA (Existing Key)** on a KMS key another CA already uses produces the same <ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">SKI </ins>collision, so <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">also </del>avoid reusing keys across CAs<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">. A per-CA CRL and the rejection of imports whose SKI collides with an existing CA are both proposed in </del>[<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">lamassuiot#834</del>](https://github.com/lamassuiot/lamassuiot/issues/834)<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">, and neither is implemented yet</del>.\n\nPeriodic and automatic regeneration [#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration]\n\nThe VA scheduled job checks <ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">the CRL settings of </ins>every <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">role</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">CA</ins> on each run and requests a new list when either of these conditions is met:\n\n* **The list is about to expire:** the remaining validity of `latest_crl` is less than the interval between job runs. This condition always applies and cannot be disabled.\n* **The refresh interval has elapsed:** the time since the issuance of the latest published CRL reaches `refresh_interval`. Setting `refresh_interval` to `0` disables only this condition; the list is still regenerated when it is about to expire.\n\nSetting `refresh_interval` to `0` therefore does not freeze regeneration. With the default 7-day validity and an hourly job, a CRL is renewed up to an hour before it expires even if `refresh_interval` is `0`. Because the job runs on its own schedule, `refresh_interval` is honored only as precisely as the job frequency allows.\n\nThe job frequency is set in Helm with `services.va.job.crl.frequency` (default `0 * * * *`) and accepts a duration (`1h`) or a cron expression. A cron expression is converted into a constant interval equal to the time left until its next firing when the service starts, so it is not aligned with the clock. Keep `validity` well above that interval: with a shorter validity, the list is regenerated on every cycle.\n\nIf generation fails (for example, because <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">of a role without a CA or an invalid signer</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">the CA cannot be resolved or the signer is invalid</ins>), the job logs the warning <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">`something went wrong while calculating CRL for CA <ski>`</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">`something went wrong while calculating CRL for CA <ca-id>`</ins> and retries on the next cycle. There is no other renewal path until the cause is fixed, and the last published list keeps being served until it expires. All of this depends on working jobs, storage and signing.\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Settings whose `status` is `ARCHIVED` are skipped: nothing regenerates their CRL.\n</div>\n\nWith `regenerate_on_revoke`, the event handler requests a new CRL upon revocation or reactivation from `CertificateHold` to `ACTIVE`. This happens after the status change and has its own failure paths: if generation fails (for example, because of an invalid signer), the event is retried and, once retries are exhausted, moves to the failed-events queue. If the issuing CA has no <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">VA role</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">CRL settings</ins>, there is no CRL to regenerate: the event is acknowledged without retry, the VA logs a warning, and nothing appears in the failed-events queue. Confirming revocation does not establish that the new CRL is already published or that all clients have downloaded it.\n\nCompare CRL numbers and timestamps before and after a change. Monitor expired lists and generation failures; clients must apply policy when current evidence is unavailable.\n\nCRL contents [#crl-contents]\n\nEach entry contains serial number, revocation time and reason. The generator queries certificates in `REVOKED` state for the selected CA. A reactivated certificate disappears when the list is recalculated; an earlier downloaded copy retains its contents.\n\nThe list contains a critical *Issuing Distribution Point* (OID `2.5.29.28`) with configured distribution URLs. Check that your client interprets this extension and that it matches the list’s scope. If rejected, investigate format and scope; ignoring a critical extension is not successful validation.\n\nPublic download endpoint [#public-download-endpoint]\n\nThe VA service exposes <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">`GET /crl/{subject_key_id}`</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">`GET /crl/{id}`</ins> without management authorization. Use the **issuing CA’s** <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">SKI</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">ID</ins>, not the end-entity certificate’s <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">SKI</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">ID</ins> or serial<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">. Certificates issued before the 4.0.0 upgrade carry `/crl/<ski>` URLs with the SKI of the CA key; that form keeps resolving</ins>. First inspect the certificate’s CDP:\n\n```bash\nopenssl x509 -in device.crt -noout -text\n```\n\nReplace the following URL with the CDP URL and review exposure in [Deployment](/docs/deployment/self-hosted/networking):\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-final\">\n  ```bash\n  CRL_URL='http://pki.example.com/crl/REPLACE_WITH_CDP_ID'\n\n  curl --fail --show-error --silent --max-time 15 \\\n    \"$CRL_URL\" -o ca.crl.der\n\n  openssl crl -inform DER -in ca.crl.der \\\n    -outform PEM -out ca.crl.pem\n\n  openssl crl -in ca.crl.pem -noout \\\n    -issuer -lastupdate -nextupdate -crlnumber\n\n  openssl crl -in ca.crl.pem -noout -text\n  openssl crl -in ca.crl.pem -noout -verify -CAfile issuer.pem\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-merged\">\n  ```bash lmdiff=\"-+============\"\n  CRL_URL='http://pki.example.com/crl/REPLACE_WITH_ISSUER_SKI'\n  CRL_URL='http://pki.example.com/crl/REPLACE_WITH_CDP_ID'\n\n  curl --fail --show-error --silent --max-time 15 \\\n    \"$CRL_URL\" -o ca.crl.der\n\n  openssl crl -inform DER -in ca.crl.der \\\n    -outform PEM -out ca.crl.pem\n\n  openssl crl -in ca.crl.pem -noout \\\n    -issuer -lastupdate -nextupdate -crlnumber\n\n  openssl crl -in ca.crl.pem -noout -text\n  openssl crl -in ca.crl.pem -noout -verify -CAfile issuer.pem\n  ```\n</div>\n\nYou need OpenSSL 3.x and `issuer.pem` containing the direct issuer’s certificate. An accepted signer certificate has the same subject and public key as the CA, so this signature check also works when the <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">role points</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">settings point</ins> to an earlier certificate for that CA and key. Require a valid signature, current timestamps and the expected issuer. For a root that issues directly:\n\n```bash\nopenssl verify -CAfile roots.pem -CRLfile ca.crl.pem \\\n  -crl_check -purpose sslclient device.crt\n```\n\n`roots.pem` contains deliberately trusted roots. For intermediates, add `-untrusted intermediates.pem` before the `device.crt` argument. `-crl_check` checks the end-entity certificate’s revocation against the local list; it does not download other lists or establish the status of the whole chain. A complete chain check needs the corresponding CRLs and a policy requiring them.\n\nThe test should accept an allowed certificate absent from the list and reject it with `certificate revoked` after publishing and using the list containing its serial.\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-del lm-diff-block\">\n  VA role management API\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  CRL settings API [#crl-settings-api]\n</div>\n\nThe following paths are relative to the VA service; confirm the public prefix in the Gateway. They are not the URLs of the public OCSP and CRL endpoints.\n\n| Method | Relative path                                                                                                        | Operation                                                                                                              |\n| ------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `GET`  | `/v1/crl-settings`                                                                                                   | List the CRL settings; filterable at least by `ca_id`, `status` and `legacy_ski`.                                      |\n| `GET`  | <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">`/v1/roles/{ca-ski}`</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">`/v1/crl-settings/{ca-id}`</ins> | Read <ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">the </ins>`crl_options` and `latest_crl`<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\"> of a CA</ins>. |\n| `PUT`  | <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">`/v1/roles/{ca-ski}`</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">`/v1/crl-settings/{ca-id}`</ins> | Replace <ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">the </ins>CRL configuration<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\"> of a CA</ins>.           |\n\nYou need authentication and <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">role </del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">CRL settings </ins>read or update permissions. Consult your installation’s [API reference](/docs/api-reference) and [Access control](/docs/platform/administration/access-control). Read current configuration before updating and send the full object expected by the contract<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\"> — `refresh_interval`, `validity`, `signer_serial_number` and `regenerate_on_revoke`</ins>: the backend replaces <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">`crl_options`</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">all four</ins>; it does not merge omitted fields<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">. `status`, `legacy_ski` and `latest_crl` are read-only. A `PUT` returns 409 when the settings are not `ACTIVE`, validates the signer only when it changes, and does not regenerate the CRL</ins>.\n\nAfter a change, check saved configuration and the next list actually published. Updating the <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">role</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">settings</ins> alone does not establish regeneration or client acceptance.\n\nCommon VA roleCRL settings errors [#common-crl-settings-errors]\n\n| Symptom                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | Cause                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Action                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">`PUT /v1/roles/{ca-ski}` returns 404 and `GET`</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">`PUT /v1/crl-settings/{ca-id}`</ins> returns <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">200</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">409</ins>. | The <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">role’s CA was deleted and no other CA with the same SKI remains</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">settings are not `ACTIVE`: the migration archived them because their CA was deleted before the upgrade</ins>.                                             | There is no API way <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">out; it requires database intervention. Avoid it by adjusting the role before deleting the CA</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">back to `ACTIVE`. The last CRL keeps being served</ins>.                                               |\n| `PUT` returns 400.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | The <ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">new </ins>signer lacks `cRLSign`, or its subject and <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">key match no CA with that SKI (for example, after deleting the first of two CAs that share a key)</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">public key do not match the CA</ins>. | <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">Use `PUT` to set</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">Set</ins> the serial number of a valid certificate of <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">the remaining</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">that</ins> CA.                                                       |\n| `PUT` returns 404.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | The CA or the new signer certificate does not exist (for example, the CA was deleted), or the CA has no CRL settings (for example, an imported CA).                                                                                                                                          | Check the CA ID and the signer serial number.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| `PUT` returns 400 from a 3.x client.                                                                                                                                                                                                     | `crl_options.subject_key_id_signer` was replaced by <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">`signer_certificate_serial_number`</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">`crl_options.signer_serial_number`</ins>, which is required.                                                                           | Update the client to send the new field.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| The CRL does not change after revoking a certificate.                                                                                                                                                                                    | `regenerate_on_revoke` is `false`, the issuing CA has no <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">VA role</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">CRL settings</ins>, or generation failed because of an invalid signer.                                                                                       | Check the VA logs. <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">A missing role only produces</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">Missing settings only produce</ins> a warning (no retry, no failed-events entry); an invalid signer also leaves the event in the failed-events queue, so fix the signer. |\n| A verifier rejects the CRL because of the issuer.                                                                                                                                                                                        | <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">Several CAs share an SKI</del><ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">The list was fetched through a legacy `/crl/<ski>` URL and belongs to the CA that owned that SKI when the upgrade ran</ins>.                                                                         | <del className=\"lm-diff-del\">See </del>[<del className=\"lm-diff-del\">CAs that share a key</del>](#cas-that-share-a-key)<ins className=\"lm-diff-ins\">Use the CDP of the certificate being validated: certificates issued in 4.0.0 carry `/crl/<ca-id>` URLs</ins>.                       |\n\nFrom the interface [#from-the-interface]\n\nThe CA view lets you inspect and download the available list. Compare version, issuer and timestamps with the copy downloaded from the public endpoint. The CA’s VA settings also let you select the CRL signer: the certificate picker only offers certificates that have a private key and carry the `cRLSign` key usage. If a configured signer’s certificate cannot be resolved, the interface shows `Unknown Certificate (Serial: X)` or `Certificate details unavailable (Serial: X)`. Diagnose differences using [PKI troubleshooting](/docs/platform/pki/troubleshooting).\n\nReferences [#references]\n\n* [RFC 5280: X.509 and CRL profile](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5280).\n* [OpenSSL: openssl-crl](https://docs.openssl.org/3.6/man1/openssl-crl/).\n* [OpenSSL: openssl-verify](https://docs.openssl.org/3.6/man1/openssl-verify/).\n\n<Cards>\n  <Card title=\"OCSP service\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/ocsp\" />\n\n  <Card title=\"Certificate validation\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/certificate-validation\" />\n</Cards>\n"]
